00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.250
So why does Spanner exist?

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.710
Let's go back to 2005.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.230
Every dollar that Google
generated from its advertising

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.410
system AdWords was on the back
of a manually sharded MySQL

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:08.490
database.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.280
That database was sharded
according to customer IDs

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.400
and occasionally we'd have
to reshard the database.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.800
We did a reshard
and that reshard

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.302
actually took multiple
years to complete.

00:01:19.302 --> 00:01:21.510
It was at that point we
decided that there's probably

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.474
a better way.

00:01:22.474 --> 00:01:24.015
So we started
development of Spanner.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.370
So as we were looking at the
requirements for the AdWords

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:33.260
system what were the
things that Google needed?

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.380
Google needed a horizontally
scalable database.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:37.610
We had lots of customers.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.110
We were growing quickly.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.240
We had gone from a
$10 million business

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.220
to over a billion
dollar a year business

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.900
and we needed to
support that scale.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.780
So we needed the
horizontal scalability.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.540
The second thing is we were
dealing with people's money.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.650
We had budgets.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:52.400
We had customers.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.150
We had partners.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:54.350
We had agencies.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.570
We had to have
asset transactions

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.040
with global consistency.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.520
We couldn't accept
eventual consistency

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.344
or any loss of data.

00:02:03.344 --> 00:02:05.510
So we had to make sure that
it was always consistent

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.760
and we always had
asset transactions.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.930
The third thing was every minute
that the AdWord system was down

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:15.470
for maintenance was millions of
dollars of revenue for Google.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.020
And so we had to have a
system that made sure that we

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:19.740
didn't have any downtime.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:20.990
So these are the requirements.

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.360
And if you look at
this today, prior

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.980
to Cloud Spanner
entering the market

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.937
there was no good solution that
met all these requirements.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.270
That brings us to Cloud Spanner.

00:02:39.804 --> 00:02:40.470
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.780
- We demand a lot
of our databases.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.560
And our databases
demand a lot of us.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.110
They're fragile, expensive,
and tough to maintain.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.100
When things break it can be hard
to put the pieces back together

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.850
at the speed of your business.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.670
And you often lose
data in the process.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.520
We've all come to expect the
distributed databases can't

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.520
be globally consistent
and scalable.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.950
But what if you didn't
have to make trade-offs?

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.530
What if you could have a
fully managed database service

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.720
that's consistent,
scales horizontally,

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.750
across data centers,
and speaks SQL?

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:16.930
This is Cloud Spanner, a mission
critical relational database

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.290
service built from the
ground up and battle

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.660
tested at Google for
strong consistency

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.470
and high availability
at global scale.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.760
At Google we use
Spanner for services

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.500
that billions of people
access every day.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.740
It's the only Cloud
service designed

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.870
to be a massively
distributed relational

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:38.320
database with transactions
and SQL semantics.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.150
And we're talking massive.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:43.330
Spanner can scale up to millions
of machines across hundreds

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.420
of data centers and trillions
of database rows stretching

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.520
across the globe while
behaving with like it's

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:52.270
all in one place,
where you want it.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:55.540
We built Cloud Spanner to
be fully managed and secure.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.000
Automatic sharding and
synchronous replication

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.240
with low latency and schema
updates without downtime

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.780
means your data is highly
available and reliable.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.870
Instead of endless provisioning
and worrying about maintenance

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:11.110
you can focus on
growing your business.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.000
No matter your
needs, you don't have

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.250
to hit a wall with
your database service.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.990
Get started with Cloud Spanner.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.079
And build what's next.

