00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.800
DOMINIC PREUSS: Great so that
is our quick introduction

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.310
into what is Cloud Spanner.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.740
So Cloud Spanner is Google's
mission critical scalable

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.580
relational database service.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.840
So almost 10 years after
the internal Spanner project

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.550
went into productions
at Google, we've

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:43.940
been able to bring that
same level of performance

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:47.420
and consistency and scalability
to our Google Cloud platform

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:48.920
customers.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.640
So what does that really mean.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.210
So first of all Cloud Spanner
is a fully managed database

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:54.270
service.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.740
So what that means is you don't
have to worry about upgrades.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:00.450
You don't have to worry
about security patches.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:03.805
You don't have to worry about
adding storage to your storage

00:05:03.805 --> 00:05:05.930
array for the next set of
data that's going to come

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.250
in your database system.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.100
All of that is handled for
you by our site Reliability

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:14.870
Engineering team who's making
sure 24/7 that that service is

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:18.190
up and running and that
we're meeting your needs.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:22.160
Now we get fairly
pedantic inside of Google

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.380
around consistency.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.870
And so when we say
asset transactions

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:28.850
and we mean global
consistency we

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:32.090
mean externalize
external serializability.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:34.602
So it's the highest
standard of consistency.

00:05:34.602 --> 00:05:36.560
There's a lot of back
and forth in the industry

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.200
about what is
eventual consistency,

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.390
or what is global consistency.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.270
We take a very high bar on this.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:45.260
And Spanner meets that need.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:48.020
So this means that you
have asset transactions

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.880
across many rows.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:51.420
You have relational semantics.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.170
So if you're coming off of
a manually sharded MySQL

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:59.000
database or a postgres cluster
or some other SQL solution

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.530
everything looks familiar.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:05.030
So your schemas are defined
in DDL so standard SQL

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.310
with SQL data types.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:11.460
You are able to do queries based
on your joins and everything

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.220
you're used to having in SQL.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:16.640
In fact, Cloud Spanner
uses the same SQL dialect

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.560
as what we've rolled
out in BigQuery, which

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:22.250
is based on Anti-SQL
2011 with some extensions

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:24.230
for the additional features
that Google offers.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.210
So with the standard
SQL and it's

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:28.490
the same SQL that you get
across the Google Cloud

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:33.390
platform on our data
warehousing product BigQuery.

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:35.290
It's automatic
synchronous replication.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:38.410
So today in the beta, we've
rolled out regional versions

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:39.880
of Cloud Spanner.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:43.150
So what that means is we
have multiple replicas

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:46.360
across multiple zones
inside of a single region.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:50.417
And this gives you four
nines of availability.

00:06:50.417 --> 00:06:52.000
It also means that
when you write data

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.430
into Spanner in
the Cloud Spanner,

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:55.870
you know that it
has been written.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:57.370
If we've given you
an acknowledgment

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:59.260
that that transaction
has committed,

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:02.770
then you know that
data is there in any

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:04.690
reads that you do
after that time

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.150
stamp will include that data.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:09.249
We give these guarantees
so that you always

00:07:09.249 --> 00:07:11.290
know that you're getting
the most consistent view

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:13.960
of your data at all times, or
a consistent view of your data

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:15.550
at all times.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:17.950
Later in the year we'll be
rolling out multi-regional.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:21.310
So multi-regional means that you
can actually span your database

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:24.250
across multiple Google
Cloud Platform regions

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:26.830
to achieve an even higher
level of availability.

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:28.870
So multi-regional
Cloud Spanner instances

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:31.540
will offer five nines
of availability.

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:33.970
So what that means is you're
talking about 20 seconds

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:36.460
a month, I'm sorry two
seconds a month of downtime

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:38.577
with five nines.

00:07:38.577 --> 00:07:39.910
And this has been battle tested.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:41.710
And the reason that
I can get up on stage

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.960
and make these
types of claims is

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.684
because we know that we've
done this internally.

00:07:46.684 --> 00:07:48.100
So we've been doing
this at Google

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:51.310
for almost 10 years with
the internal Spanner system.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.800
And so we know that when
we make these guarantees

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:56.921
of availability and
consistency and durability,

00:07:56.921 --> 00:07:58.420
we can stand behind
them, because we

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:01.630
have almost 10 years of
battle tested experience

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:05.065
running it internally.

00:08:05.065 --> 00:08:06.940
Google Cloud Spanner
has been used internally

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:08.856
for many of our mission
critical applications.

00:08:08.856 --> 00:08:11.980
So we've talked about Google
AdWords, the Google Play Store,

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:13.630
and in fact Google
Cloud Platform

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:17.260
itself is built on
top of Cloud Spanner.

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.840
So the control plane that
manages all of the VMs

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.300
and all of the services
across Google Cloud platform

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.130
is actually based on Spanner.

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:26.500
So you can have confidence
that when you put your data

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.480
and build your application
on the top of Cloud Spanner

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.646
you're getting the best of
what Google has to offer.

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:36.490
So Dominic, you just
said a bunch of words.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:37.780
What does that mean?

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:40.250
How does that compare
to other solutions?

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:44.110
So if you compare it to a
traditional relational database

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:49.510
with traditional RDBMS,
we have strong schemas.

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:53.200
We have SQL schemas
and querying.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.750
We have strong consistency.

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:58.360
We have high availability.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.550
Quite often I get asked,
how does Cloud Spanner

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:04.300
compare to the RDBMS that
I'm using either on premise

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:05.860
or in another public cloud?

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:09.080
And invariably they're trying
to compare a single node

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:13.360
solution to Spanner, which
is a distributed system.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.720
So when you have a single node
master slave configuration,

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.400
you're dependent
on the master node.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.520
That means that you can
only do as many rights

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.230
as you can put through
the master node.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:27.040
And if the master node needs
to be updated, either get

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:29.670
a new version of software,
get a security patch,

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.560
need to do any maintenance, you
either have to take the master

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.600
node down and take
down the whole system,

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:37.200
or failover your master
node to your slaves

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:39.250
you can do maintenance
on the master.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:40.990
What this means is
that your application

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:43.090
will incur downtime.

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:45.460
Now that downtime
might be scheduled.

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:46.930
But it's still downtime.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:48.460
For Google it doesn't matter.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:50.350
We're selling ads
around the world.

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:52.507
We can't schedule downtime.

00:09:52.507 --> 00:09:54.340
We're making money and
we have a system that

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:56.530
is reliant on the system 24/7.

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:59.170
So we can't have
downtime, either scheduled

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:00.220
or unscheduled.

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:02.800
So Cloud Spanner, because
of its distributed nature,

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.751
means that we can do
updates, security updates,

00:10:05.751 --> 00:10:07.750
any maintenance that we
need to do in the system

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:10.330
by moving your data around
on the multiple nodes.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:11.860
And you don't see any downtime.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:14.830
In fact you see nothing
on the performance side

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:16.349
of what you experience.

00:10:16.349 --> 00:10:17.890
You'll see consistent
experience even

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:20.900
when we're doing things like
upgrades and security patches.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.940
So in that sense, it's
horizontally scalable.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:29.070
So the way that you
achieve additional scale

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:31.650
is you add nodes, which
is what you would normally

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.560
see a NoSQL database, which
we'll talk about in a second.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.900
So instead of relying on
vertical scalability, which

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.810
means that if I need more
throughput through my master

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.540
slave, I just put
it on a bigger box.

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:43.650
I give it more RAM.

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:45.500
I give it more memory.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:48.630
And so that's the
vertical scaling strategy.

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:52.890
And so with Spanner, we give
you horizontal scalability.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:56.820
So you don't worry about I'm
on the biggest VM I really

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.043
hope this provider makes a
bigger VM because when I'm

00:10:59.043 --> 00:11:00.420
going to run out of size soon.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.836
Right, you know
that we can always

00:11:01.836 --> 00:11:05.490
add additional capacity so that
you can grow your application.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:10.080
Also replication, so traditional
RDBMSs, one of the ways

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.250
that you can scale
out horizontally

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:15.090
is you can shard your
read and write workloads.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:18.900
So you can add additional
read replicas to your system.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.020
And then you can do your reads
off of those read replicas.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:26.010
Therefore giving additional
capacity to your master node.

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.320
Now the problem there
is that those reads

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:32.130
of the read replicas are stale.

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:34.890
There is a replication
lag for the data that's

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:36.390
making that read replica.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:38.190
And so it's up to you
and your application

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:40.860
to know that you may or
may not have the most

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:42.450
consistent version of the data.

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:44.700
If you need a
consistent read you

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.730
have to go back to the master
node and therefore using up

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:51.310
some those resources and take
away some of your scalability.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:54.480
So that's how it relates
to a traditional RDBMS.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.070
How does it relate to NoSQL?

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:58.680
So the pattern that we
would see over and over

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.990
and over again with
our customers is,

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:02.910
both our enterprise
and our start-ups

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:04.410
and smaller companies,
is that they

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:07.320
would build an application
using a traditional RDBMS.

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:09.300
It might be MySQL,
might be postgres,

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:11.720
might be name your
commercial database.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:14.250
And then what happens
is they just grew.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.920
And we all know that data
is growing exponentially.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:17.970
It is growing faster--

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.800
at a faster rate than its
ever grown in our history.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:24.240
And so the requirements that
we have in our databases

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:25.780
continues to grow.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:29.250
And so often what happens
is a company or project

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:31.860
has this relational database
that backs their application.

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:34.440
And they hit the upper bound
of what they can do for scale.

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:36.840
So there are only
immutable requirement

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.850
is horizontal scalability.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:43.070
So they then go and choose
a NoSQL solution, Cassandra,

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:44.890
Maunganui, whatever
it happens to be.

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:48.090
And they basically
trade global consistency

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:50.370
for horizontal scalability.

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:52.830
Because that is their
immutable requirement.

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:54.960
And then they write a
bunch of application logic.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.120
Or use other tools
to try to get around

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:00.810
the transactional semantics
of having consistency only

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:03.732
at a shard level, or not
across the entire database.

00:13:03.732 --> 00:13:05.190
So what Spanner
allows you to do is

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:07.110
to not have to make this trade.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:09.450
Now if you've
outgrown your RDBMS

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:11.820
Cloud Spanner is not
a drop in replacement.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:13.990
It's not a SQL
compatible database.

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:15.675
It's not a postgres
compatible database.

00:13:15.675 --> 00:13:17.550
So there is going to be
work for you required

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.880
to migrate your traditional
RDBMS application on the Cloud

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:21.570
Spanner.

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:24.220
But you get to know that
when you make that transition

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:25.650
you don't have to
make a trade off

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:28.815
between horizontal scalability
and global consistency.

00:13:35.059 --> 00:13:36.600
I'm often asked,
what was the hardest

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:39.660
thing about taking the
internal Spanner application

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:42.810
and exposing it to our Google
Cloud Platform customers.

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:45.000
And the answer is
making it open.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:47.580
So Google is committed
to being the open Cloud.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:49.170
And so we've done
everything we can

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:53.760
to adopt open standards and
existing protocols and APIs

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:55.235
that exist in the outside world.

00:13:55.235 --> 00:13:56.610
We aren't trying
to move everyone

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.170
on to Google
proprietary technology.

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:00.720
We're trying to move and
solve customers' needs

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:02.932
where they are, by
supporting those standards.

00:14:02.932 --> 00:14:04.890
And so Cloud Spanners
spent a tremendous amount

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:07.390
of time trying to make
sure that this was true.

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:09.450
So we started with standard SQL.

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.550
We've had a number of
different SQL dialects

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.740
across the Google
Cloud Platform.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.510
And so we decided
that we needed to come

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:18.900
to a standard SQL dialect
across all Google products.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:22.790
So we took Ansi-SQL 2011 as a
spec and then extended that.

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:27.180
And so what this means is
when you look at SQL Spanner,

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.900
SQL in Spanner you
see normal SQL data

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:33.996
types and the types
of queries and joins

00:14:33.996 --> 00:14:35.370
and things that
you would expect.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:37.890
So we make it as easy as
possible for customers

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:41.730
by reducing the friction
in the impedance mismatch

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:45.040
between their existing solution.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:47.520
The second thing is
we spent a lot of time

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:50.220
on enterprise requirements.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:53.460
Cloud Spanner actually launched
with the best integration

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:56.490
across all of our various
cross-platform initiatives

00:14:56.490 --> 00:14:59.800
for identity and access
management encryption and auto

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:00.300
logging.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:03.630
It came out of the gate in
beta with all of the features

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:06.720
that traditional enterprise
that has high security needs

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:08.620
would need to be able
to adopt this product.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:10.950
And so we spent a lot of
time investing in that,

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:13.075
and making sure that the
product had those features

00:15:13.075 --> 00:15:15.400
from the very beginning.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:17.650
We also spent a lot of
time in client libraries.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:19.960
So we'd like to talk about
being a very developer

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:22.150
friendly platform.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:25.210
And being developer
friendly means two things.

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.130
I mean you've got
great documents

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:28.600
and great documentation.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:30.860
And you have really
strong client libraries.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:33.400
So we spent a ton of time
working with our early access

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:34.330
customers.

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:37.450
Cloud Spanner was in early
access for over 12 months.

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:38.950
And what we were
doing in that time

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:42.130
was onboarding customers,
showing the documents,

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:44.110
finding the holes in the
places that we forgot

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:46.180
to document things,
adding those documents

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:47.650
and really doing
lots of iterations

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:50.470
on the documentation, so that
when we launched we actually

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:52.801
had all of that ready
for our developers.

00:15:52.801 --> 00:15:54.550
The second thing that
was really important

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:56.000
was our client libraries.

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:59.890
So we spent a ton of time
on our Java, Python, Go

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:02.170
and node.js client
libraries to make sure

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.610
that they were fully baked.

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:05.710
They were optimized
for performance.

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:08.140
We obscured any of
the common gotchas

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:11.110
that you would have if you were
trying to use the API directly

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:13.450
in an idiomatic way
for the language.

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:17.290
So we spent a ton of time
working with the various teams

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:20.410
to make sure that we had a
first class developer client

00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:23.140
library in these languages.

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:25.450
The last thing is JDBC.

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:27.130
In case you haven't
heard this yet,

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:29.800
the partner ecosystem is
incredibly important to Google.

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:31.300
And so one of the
things that we did

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:34.300
to make it as easy as possible
for both customers to adopt

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:37.930
and partners, is that we
launched with a JDBC driver.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:40.420
The JDBC driver is read-only.

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:42.490
And what that means is
that we can hand that

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:45.670
to a number of our partners
or your internal customers.

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:48.909
And they have a fully
understood client library

00:16:48.909 --> 00:16:50.950
that they can often plug-in
with very little work

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:52.240
to their frameworks.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:54.340
And this allowed us
to launch integrations

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:57.790
with a number of partners early
because we were able to deliver

00:16:57.790 --> 00:16:59.140
them a JDBC driver.

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:01.420
And that was able to
decrease the time necessary

00:17:01.420 --> 00:17:04.230
for them to implement
access in the Cloud Spanner.

00:17:08.329 --> 00:17:12.410
So what types of workloads are
appropriate for Cloud Spanner?

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:15.349
So as we were going through
the early access program

00:17:15.349 --> 00:17:17.599
we saw a number
of patterns emerge

00:17:17.599 --> 00:17:20.839
from the types of use cases that
people found for Cloud Spanner.

00:17:20.839 --> 00:17:23.000
So the first one
was transactional.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.220
If you have an
application that requires

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:28.380
transactional consistency
or complex transactions,

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:30.440
then that is an ideal
use case for Spanner.

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:33.350
So if you're dealing with
things like inventory management

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:36.800
where you can't sell more
products than you have,

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:40.169
or if you are trying to
manage financial transactions

00:17:40.169 --> 00:17:42.710
you have to make sure that you
always have the right amounts.

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:45.980
And you have to have really
strong transactional semantics

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:47.520
to be able to support that.

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:50.690
So what we saw is a
number of the companies

00:17:50.690 --> 00:17:54.890
that we've worked with had
a manually sharded system,

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:57.170
or had built a
transactional system

00:17:57.170 --> 00:18:00.740
on top of their manually
sharded or eventually consistent

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:03.830
Datastore to make sure
that they were guaranteeing

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:05.900
transactional consistency.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:07.430
So what Spanner
allows you to do is

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:10.820
to push all of the
transactional code

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:12.890
and all of the responsibilities
for maintaining

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:14.912
transactional consistency
into the database.

00:18:14.912 --> 00:18:16.370
So what this means
is that you have

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:19.430
much simpler architectures,
much higher velocity

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:22.400
for your developers, less code
that can go wrong and introduce

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.860
bugs, because you can
rely on the database

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:26.720
itself for maintaining
and providing

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:29.810
all of your transactional
consistency.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:31.250
The second one was scaleout.

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:35.030
And people often assume that
unless they have tons of data

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:36.410
they can't use Spanner.

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:39.560
And the answer is if you have
mission critical system that

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:41.780
requires high
availability then Spanner

00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:43.260
can be a great fit for you.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:47.570
If you have 10 gigabytes of
data or you have a pentabyte

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:49.190
of data, it doesn't matter.

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:51.350
You have a mission
critical application.

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:56.000
And so Cloud Spanner gives
you a Cloud native mission

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:59.210
critical database that
also provides scale.

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:02.750
So if you have hit the upper
bounds of what you can do

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:06.380
and we continue to see other
Cloud providers and database

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:08.960
providers rolling out kind
of incremental changes

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:11.000
in the amount of data
that they can store.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:12.980
What Spanner does is
allows you to have

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:15.020
a non-incremental change.

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:18.650
We go from being able to
store specific single digit

00:19:18.650 --> 00:19:21.560
terabytes of data into
as much data as you have.

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:25.370
So if you happen to have
a very large customer set

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:27.410
or large number of
transactions or a lot of data

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:29.727
that you have to have
transactional consistency,

00:19:29.727 --> 00:19:32.060
then Spanner allows you to
meet that need without having

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:34.910
to take any trade-offs.

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:37.880
Another common use case
is Global Data Plane.

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:40.190
And this is very common
inside of Google and actually

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:42.270
the Google Cloud
Platform use case.

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:43.996
So if you have--

00:19:43.996 --> 00:19:45.620
let's say that you
have user bases that

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:46.640
are around the world.

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:48.360
You have a set of
users in North America,

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.690
a set of users in Europe,
and a set of users in Asia.

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:53.600
And you want to keep all
of the user data close

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:55.400
to the customers
then you can do that.

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:57.920
You can have a regional
instance or an RDBMS in the US,

00:19:57.920 --> 00:19:59.870
one in Europe and one in Asia.

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:01.430
But what you need
to know is have

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:04.250
a single global
system that tells you

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:05.870
where that data lives.

00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:09.820
So for this user, user
ABC, I know that data

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:12.320
that user is homed in Europe.

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:14.540
So I'm going to go to
the European instance

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:16.230
to go ahead and get that data.

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:17.870
And so these Global
Control Plane

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:20.180
where it might be a very
small amount of data.

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:22.490
But you have to be able to
query it from everywhere,

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:24.770
have low latency and
know that you have

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:27.964
a consistent view the data is
a very common use case we saw

00:20:27.964 --> 00:20:29.630
both inside of Google
and we're starting

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:33.290
to see more commonly
in the outside world.

00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:35.630
The last one is
database consolidation.

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:38.070
So everyone knows
the story, right

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:40.670
you keep your user data
in MySQL or postgres.

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:43.400
You keep all your event and
logs data in a Cassandra cluster

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:44.450
or in BigQuery.

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:46.070
And then you ETL
the data around.

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:49.850
Right every night you copy
a copy of your user table

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:52.101
into BigQuery so you
can do joins against it.

00:20:52.101 --> 00:20:53.600
What we're seeing
with Cloud Spanner

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:55.610
is it allows you to
consolidate your databases

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:57.417
into a single source of truth.

00:20:57.417 --> 00:20:59.000
So you could take
your user data which

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:00.755
that might be in
postgres or MySQL,

00:21:00.755 --> 00:21:02.630
and you can merge it
with all the events data

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:05.570
that you have in Cassandra
or Maunganui or BigQuery.

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:07.730
And you can combine them
into a single database.

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:09.421
You no longer have
to ETL data around.

00:21:09.421 --> 00:21:11.420
You don't have to worry
about staleness of data.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:12.770
You can do joins
across all the data

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:14.978
and you know that you have
a single consistent source

00:21:14.978 --> 00:21:16.910
of truth for all of that data.

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:20.924
Again it is a tremendous
gain in productivity

00:21:20.924 --> 00:21:22.340
for your development
teams if they

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:24.417
don't have to worry about
multiple data stores.

00:21:24.417 --> 00:21:26.000
If you can put all
of your data in one

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.400
place, and Cloud Spanner
makes sense for that.

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:30.960
It removes tremendous
complexity from your stack.

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:32.720
So that removes bugs.

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:35.570
It makes it easier to maintain
code, increases developer

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:36.295
velocity.

00:21:36.295 --> 00:21:37.670
And this is again
something we've

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:39.050
seen a lot inside of Google.

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:41.383
And we're starting to see
with our Google Cloud platform

00:21:41.383 --> 00:21:43.880
customers.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:45.550
So how does Spanner do this?

00:21:45.550 --> 00:21:47.650
I say that it's
horizontally scalable.

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:49.210
So what does that mean?

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:52.735
So for Cloud Spanner you start
with a Cloud Spanner instance.

00:21:52.735 --> 00:21:55.360
And Robert is going to demo how
to actually create one of these

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:56.785
and you can see how easy it is.

00:21:56.785 --> 00:21:58.660
But you create a Cloud
Spanner, for instance,

00:21:58.660 --> 00:22:00.370
and when you create
that instance

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:03.400
you basically tell us what you
want your replication topology

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:04.250
to look like.

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:06.220
So if you're using a
regional Spanner instance,

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:09.870
we have multiple replicas
in multiple zones

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:11.340
inside of a single region.

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:13.770
And then each of
those replicas has

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:17.100
its own set of compute
resources and storage.

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:20.050
And then you put your database
inside of that instance.

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:23.490
So an instance can
have 100 databases.

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:25.880
And then each database
can have 2,000 tables.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:28.620
And each of those tables can
have as many rows as you want.

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:31.320
And so what this means is
that your data is actually

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:34.170
replicated across
each of the replicas.

00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:37.500
And we've got full copies of
all the data in all the places.

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:39.570
And so there is
multiple replicas

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:42.719
running at any given time.

00:22:42.719 --> 00:22:44.760
So what happens is if
somebody comes in and wants

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:46.360
to make an update.

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:49.020
So let's say that you want to
update a row in a database.

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:51.150
Or let's say you
want update 100 rows.

00:22:51.150 --> 00:22:52.620
That update comes
into the system

00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:55.650
and goes into one
of the replicas.

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:58.540
This is where Spanner
magic starts to happen.

00:22:58.540 --> 00:23:01.260
That update comes
into the system.

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:04.890
We know that each of the
different replicas has parts.

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:06.390
They have all the data.

00:23:06.390 --> 00:23:08.490
But we know that certain
parts of the data

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:10.124
are owned by different nodes.

00:23:10.124 --> 00:23:11.790
So when that update
comes in, we come in

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:13.734
and we notify the
appropriate nodes.

00:23:13.734 --> 00:23:15.150
And then that data
gets replicated

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:17.220
across all of the replicas.

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:19.980
We use a custom
Paxos algorithm that

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:23.430
allows us to do consensus
based writes on the system.

00:23:23.430 --> 00:23:26.220
And this is what allows us to
have our global consistency.

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:28.980
So we have a custom
Paxos algorithm.

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:30.990
And we have two-phase
commits which

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:34.650
allows us to have global
consistency across the database

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:35.730
at all times.

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:37.950
We try to do everything
without the two-phase commit.

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:39.408
But if you actually
are joining it,

00:23:39.408 --> 00:23:41.700
you're trying to update
data across multiple nodes

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:43.470
that we fall back
to two-phased commit

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:46.578
to make sure that we don't lose
consistency of the database.

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:55.799
This is all well and good
and Spanner sounds magic.

00:23:55.799 --> 00:23:57.340
But there has to be
some differences.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:59.780
There can't just be a
drop in replacement.

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:01.100
And the answer is you're right.

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:04.880
So to get the advantages
of a distributed system

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:07.310
we impose certain
design constraints

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:09.170
or we impose certain concepts.

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:12.350
So the first one is the way
that we leave out the data.

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:14.900
So in a regular
relational database

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:17.960
if you had two sets of
data that were related,

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:20.690
you would have two tables
and you may or may not

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:22.760
have a foreign key constraint.

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:24.790
Which would allow if
you delete one row

00:24:24.790 --> 00:24:27.029
and it deletes all
of the children rows.

00:24:27.029 --> 00:24:28.820
And so this is the way
you would lay it out

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:30.560
in a normal relational database.

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:33.410
Postgres SQL does
support child tables,

00:24:33.410 --> 00:24:36.440
which implement some of this
but for different reasons.

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:39.200
So what we've done in
Spanner to make it performant

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:42.860
and to help customers
tell us what data should

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:47.070
be grouped together we have the
concept of interleaved tables.

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:49.160
So what this means
is that when you

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:51.620
have tables that are
dependent on each other,

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:55.250
you literally interleave them
together into the same table.

00:24:55.250 --> 00:24:59.870
So you can see our top level
row is the Beatles, where

00:24:59.870 --> 00:25:02.060
the artist's ID is number 1.

00:25:02.060 --> 00:25:06.080
And then we have albums
that are under it

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:11.300
that are from the artist
and they have IDs 1 and 2.

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:15.300
So we have a primary key
for The Beatles, which is 1,

00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:18.050
and then 1 and 2, which are
the primary keys for the child

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:18.770
table.

00:25:18.770 --> 00:25:22.940
What this tells us is that
all of the information that

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:24.790
is under Beatles is related.

00:25:24.790 --> 00:25:27.530
And so what that allows
us to do is to group

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:30.690
all of the data together
on a single node.

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:33.500
So if you commonly are
doing queries where you say,

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:35.840
show me all of the
songs for the Beatles.

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:37.754
Or show me all the
albums for the Beatles.

00:25:37.754 --> 00:25:39.170
And you know that
your queries are

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:41.420
going to be within
a subset of the data

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:44.210
by using interleaved
tables you can tell us

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:46.285
the logical grouping
of your data.

00:25:46.285 --> 00:25:47.660
And this allows
us to do a better

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:50.570
job of splitting the data
across multiple nodes, which

00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:53.780
allows us to respond to queries
more quickly because we don't

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:56.120
have to rely on
two-phase commit.

00:25:56.120 --> 00:25:58.947
We can serve all the data off
of that one section of the data

00:25:58.947 --> 00:25:59.780
in a very quick way.

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:06.310
So what does this look like
from a schema point of view?

00:26:06.310 --> 00:26:08.170
So when you defined
your schema, as we

00:26:08.170 --> 00:26:12.040
talked about we used
DDLs standard SQL create

00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:18.070
table singer's, singer ID,
is INT64 not null singer

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:20.370
name as a string max length.

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:22.330
And primary key is singer ID.

00:26:22.330 --> 00:26:25.720
So we need this primary key to
know how to split your data up

00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:27.640
and shard your
data across nodes.

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:30.820
So that if albums is a
subset of all the state

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:33.550
and is dependent on the
parent table, then we can say

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:36.880
create table albums,
singer ID INT64.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:41.200
So child tables have to
have all of the primary keys

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:42.230
of its parent.

00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:44.050
So it has to have singer ID.

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:49.150
And then we've added album
ID as a second primary key.

00:26:49.150 --> 00:26:51.315
And we also have an album
name which is string.

00:26:51.315 --> 00:26:52.690
And then when we
define the table

00:26:52.690 --> 00:26:54.880
we say interleave in singers.

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:59.380
And so this is the concept
of foreign key constraints

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:01.510
that Spanner supports.

00:27:01.510 --> 00:27:03.610
It's not arbitrary
foreign key constraints

00:27:03.610 --> 00:27:06.280
because that doesn't work
on a distributed system.

00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:08.740
By telling us how to group
all the data together then

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:10.920
you can start to do things
like cascade on delete

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:12.711
and some of the other
benefits that you get

00:27:12.711 --> 00:27:14.090
from a foreign key constraint.

00:27:14.090 --> 00:27:18.320
But we only do this at a
interleave table basis.

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:21.040
The other thing that's great,
that I love telling customers

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:23.497
about, is schema migrations.

00:27:23.497 --> 00:27:25.330
So the other thing that
this allows us to do

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:27.970
is no downtime
schema migrations.

00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:30.700
So you can actually
alter your table

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:32.980
and add a column,
in this case age.

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:35.110
We can apply that
to the database.

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:40.390
And that updates the schema on
the fly so no downtime online.

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:43.430
And you know that once
that transaction--

00:27:43.430 --> 00:27:45.970
schema update is out of the
transaction, once that commits,

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:48.790
you know that all subsequent
reads after that transaction

00:27:48.790 --> 00:27:51.830
have the new data.

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:53.540
Again getting back
to the problem

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:55.400
we talked about with
AdWords, we couldn't

00:27:55.400 --> 00:27:57.530
handle either
locking the database

00:27:57.530 --> 00:27:59.840
or shutting down the database
to do schema migrations.

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:03.860
We needed to be able to do
schema migrations on the fly.

00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:06.170
So alterations to the
schema are online.

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:12.650
So what does this
look like in code?

00:28:12.650 --> 00:28:15.980
So in any of these
sessions we try to give you

00:28:15.980 --> 00:28:17.330
actionable information.

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:19.160
I'm a Python guy
so I've gone ahead

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:21.350
and pulled the Python code.

00:28:21.350 --> 00:28:25.680
So if you use our Google
Cloud client libraries.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:30.040
So we have-- we've rolled
open source client libraries

00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:32.060
in all the primary languages.

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:37.520
So if I install the Python
Google Cloud library into PIP,

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:40.590
so PIP install Google-Cloud.

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:43.640
I can then import Spanner
from Google Cloud.

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:45.660
I instantiate a client.

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:49.070
So that client allows me
to talk to the service.

00:28:49.070 --> 00:28:54.080
I then say I need my instance so
please give me my instance ID.

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:58.450
And then from the instance,
I can grab my database.

00:28:58.450 --> 00:29:00.180
So I say what does
my database ID?

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:02.790
And then I ask my
instance for the database.

00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:06.330
I now have a handle
to my database

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:08.250
where I can start
to send queries.

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:11.970
So I could do results sets
by saying database.executeSQL

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:14.070
and pass it SQL queries.

00:29:14.070 --> 00:29:16.830
And so these 10
lines of code are

00:29:16.830 --> 00:29:19.080
all that is necessary
to be able to be

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:22.950
up and running with your
Cloud Spanner instance.

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:26.274
We really tried to make
it as easy as possible.

00:29:26.274 --> 00:29:27.690
There's lots of
intermediate steps

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:29.106
that are happening
here that we've

00:29:29.106 --> 00:29:32.127
been able to obscure by
having really strong client

00:29:32.127 --> 00:29:33.960
libraries that do a lot
of the heavy lifting

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:36.570
underneath the hood for you,
things like connection pooling

00:29:36.570 --> 00:29:39.390
and things that could be gotchas
if you aren't familiar with how

00:29:39.390 --> 00:29:40.220
to use the service.

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:46.621
So right before we
get into the demo,

00:29:46.621 --> 00:29:48.370
how does this fit into
the broader schema?

00:29:48.370 --> 00:29:50.110
So Cloud Spanner
sounds fantastic.

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:51.610
Is it the solution
for everything?

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:52.720
The answer is no.

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:55.990
Cloud Spanner is one of
a portfolio of solutions

00:29:55.990 --> 00:29:58.460
that allow you to build
your application on top

00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:00.220
of Google Cloud platform.

00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:02.770
So starting with
Cloud SQL, So if you

00:30:02.770 --> 00:30:07.360
have a framework whether it be
Django or WordPress or Drupal

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:09.610
that requires
MySQL, then you can

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:14.920
use Cloud SQL to manage
that MySQL instance for you.

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:17.140
So it's a traditional
RDBMS that's

00:30:17.140 --> 00:30:19.660
being managed for you by Google.

00:30:19.660 --> 00:30:23.140
If you have a document
database workload commonly

00:30:23.140 --> 00:30:25.420
in mobile and web,
then Cloud Datastore

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:27.520
provides you that
data in a database.

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:30.192
Fully scalable, it's
shared resources,

00:30:30.192 --> 00:30:31.900
you can literally
start from zero dollars

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:34.960
a month all the way
up to SnapScale,

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:36.177
who just went public.

00:30:36.177 --> 00:30:38.260
So you can scale all the
way up on Cloud Datastore

00:30:38.260 --> 00:30:41.710
if your data is non-relational
and fits into a document

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:43.560
database workload.

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:46.940
Cloud Bigtable is our
analytics database.

00:30:46.940 --> 00:30:49.720
So this allows you to do low
latency single millisecond

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:55.240
reads and writes and really fast
table scans for your OIT time

00:30:55.240 --> 00:30:58.810
series Ad Tech data
MarTech data it's

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:01.116
a fantastic solution for that.

00:31:01.116 --> 00:31:02.740
And then BigQuery is
our data warehouse

00:31:02.740 --> 00:31:05.920
that allows you to write
all your data in, mostly

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:10.150
append, some mutation so you can
update and clean up your data.

00:31:10.150 --> 00:31:12.170
But it's really
our data warehouse.

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:14.950
So what Cloud Spanner
does in the portfolio is

00:31:14.950 --> 00:31:18.140
gives you a place that if you've
gone to MySQL in Cloud SQL

00:31:18.140 --> 00:31:20.600
and you're using that
as your solution.

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:22.597
If you need to be
able to scale past it

00:31:22.597 --> 00:31:24.430
you know that Google
provides you an option.

00:31:24.430 --> 00:31:27.160
So it really gives you
a future proof path

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:30.040
to be able to move
from your existing

00:31:30.040 --> 00:31:32.072
application on either
MySQL or postgres

00:31:32.072 --> 00:31:34.030
or whatever you happen
to be using and continue

00:31:34.030 --> 00:31:40.010
to grow out as a scaleout
relational database.

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:42.530
And with that I'm going
to bring up Robert to get

00:31:42.530 --> 00:31:44.960
started on the demo.

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:47.150
One other thing,
one of the questions

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:49.820
that I just went through,
I use a lot of words.

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:51.897
Flowcharts are usually helpful.

00:31:51.897 --> 00:31:53.480
So what we did here
is we put together

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.220
a decision tree that
allows you to decide

00:31:55.220 --> 00:31:58.485
which database service you
would use for which offering.

00:31:58.485 --> 00:32:00.860
So first of all, is your data
structured or unstructured?

00:32:00.860 --> 00:32:02.630
If your data is
unstructured you're

00:32:02.630 --> 00:32:04.790
going to put in a
Google Cloud Storage.

00:32:04.790 --> 00:32:07.130
That way you can use
it in access it there.

00:32:07.130 --> 00:32:09.620
If you need mobile SDKs
then Firebase Storage

00:32:09.620 --> 00:32:13.010
will provide you a mobile SDK
on the Google Cloud Storage.

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:16.970
If your data is structured
then the question

00:32:16.970 --> 00:32:18.320
is your data analytics.

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:20.510
Do you have an
analytics workload?

00:32:20.510 --> 00:32:22.820
In which case you'll come
over to the analytics branch.

00:32:22.820 --> 00:32:24.740
If you need low latency
and lots of mutations

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:29.000
use Cloud Bigtable, if you
don't use cloud BigQuery.

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:31.730
Coming back down the middle
if your data is relational

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:33.897
and the answer is no, then
you have Cloud Datastore.

00:32:33.897 --> 00:32:35.354
And if you have
mobile applications

00:32:35.354 --> 00:32:36.860
you have Firebase
real time database

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:41.570
that provides you
mobile SDKs for iOS Java

00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:43.157
and the mobile applications.

00:32:43.157 --> 00:32:44.990
If your data is relational
then the question

00:32:44.990 --> 00:32:46.416
is the new horizontal
scalability?

00:32:46.416 --> 00:32:48.290
If you don't need
horizontal scalability then

00:32:48.290 --> 00:32:49.760
Cloud SQL is a great
solution for you

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:51.260
and is going to be
your easiest path

00:32:51.260 --> 00:32:52.580
to building an application.

00:32:52.580 --> 00:32:54.350
If you're doing
horizontal scalability

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:56.477
Cloud Spanner is there
to meet your needs.

00:32:56.477 --> 00:32:58.560
So this is a quick way for
you to kind of navigate

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:01.160
which the various database
technologies will serve

00:33:01.160 --> 00:33:04.970
the need for your application.

00:33:04.970 --> 00:33:08.770
And lastly before we get to
the demo, as I said before,

00:33:08.770 --> 00:33:10.850
partner integration is
very important to us.

00:33:10.850 --> 00:33:13.250
It's why we spend a lot of
time on the JDBC driver.

00:33:13.250 --> 00:33:16.760
So we're very excited to have
xplenty, Zoomdata, looker

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:18.650
and MicroStrategy and iCharts.

00:33:18.650 --> 00:33:21.446
They all are in the
process of launching

00:33:21.446 --> 00:33:23.570
or have already launched
support for Cloud Spanner.

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:26.852
So some of your favorite BI
tools are already integrated.

00:33:26.852 --> 00:33:28.310
And we're working
with informatica,

00:33:28.310 --> 00:33:30.459
alooma, Tableau,
they've started work

00:33:30.459 --> 00:33:33.000
and they'll be rolling out their
solutions later in the year.

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:36.260
So again, if you are already
using Tableau or looker or one

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:37.924
of these solutions
in your stack we're

00:33:37.924 --> 00:33:39.590
going to make sure
that we're integrated

00:33:39.590 --> 00:33:41.840
from the very beginning to
make it as easy as possible

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:45.630
to migrate data in and
out of Cloud Spanner.

00:33:45.630 --> 00:33:47.120
So with that I
give it to Robert.

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:49.160
ROBERT KUBIS: All right,
